{"text": "Grace learns most successfully through model, guided practice, independent try; frequent cumulative review; functional links to daily life; and visual checklist with completed steps. These strategies make instruction clearer and reduce the amount of energy spent simply gaining access.\n\nThe profile also identifies predictable barriers. Grace may struggle with abstract vocabulary; remembering multi-step procedures; reading and mathematics at Grade 6 complexity; and transferring skills to new contexts. We should not treat these points as a list of personal deficits. Each one directs the team toward a response. For example, a barrier caused by pace may require additional processing time; an environmental barrier may require a quieter, safer, or more accessible setting; and task complexity may require modeling, chunking, direct instruction, or an alternate response.\n\nThe goal is not to remove every productive challenge. The goal is to remove barriers that prevent the student from engaging with the intended learning. Presenter 3 will now connect the profile’s sensory, medical, behavioural, and safety information to the EA’s day-to-day responsibilities.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-K-04.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Grace", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.475, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 5}, {"text": "learns", "start": 0.4875, "end": 0.8625, "start_char": 6, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "most", "start": 0.875, "end": 1.1375, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 17}, {"text": "successfully", "start": 1.15, "end": 1.825, "start_char": 18, "end_char": 30}, {"text": "through", "start": 1.8375, "end": 2.0625, "start_char": 31, "end_char": 38}, {"text": "model", "start": 2.075, "end": 2.525, "start_char": 39, "end_char": 44}, {"text": "guided", "start": 2.7125, "end": 3.075, "start_char": 46, "end_char": 52}, {"text": "practice", "start": 3.0875, "end": 3.675, "start_char": 53, "end_char": 61}, {"text": "independent", "start": 3.875, "end": 4.5125, "sta